      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">
        <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">1. Thermal Hydrolysis Kinetics &amp; Ammonium Bisulfate Aerosols</h4>
          <p>
            Solid sulfamic acid (<em class="font-mono">H₃NSO₃</em>) provides high scale-dissolving stoichiometry without chloride ion stress. However, at temperatures above 60°C (140°F), sulfamic acid undergoes thermal hydrolysis: <em class="font-mono">H₃NSO₃ + H₂O → (NH₄)HSO₄</em>. The resulting ammonium bisulfate salt readily forms sub-micron aerosol mists (<em class="font-mono">d &lt; 1.5 µm</em>) during boiling and steam venting. When discharged into kitchen workspaces, these mists breach the 1.0 mg/m³ threshold, triggering acute upper respiratory tract irritation and corneal chemical burns.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-ink text-sm mb-2">2. Steam Inversion Dynamics &amp; Siloxane Elastomer Depolymerization</h4>
          <p>
            During normal combi cooking, positive cavity steam pressure is contained by flexible silicone bulb gaskets. During descaling, if heating elements remain energized, superheated steam generation causes an inversion of pressure across door joints. Acidic moisture attacks the siloxane (<em class="font-mono">Si-O-Si</em>) molecular backbone of the silicone gasket via acid-catalyzed hydrolytic scission. The gasket durometer hardens from 60 Shore A to over 85 Shore A, inducing permanent compression set, micro-fissuring, and catastrophic steam leakage past the perimeter.
          </p>
        </div>
